"""Words for the NHI lab book. Plain sentences, no dashes, every number traceable to nhi-lab/evidence."""

# One short note per case: what to take from it. Every number comes from the committed results.
TAKE = {
'N-R14-01': 'A scan is only as good as the list of places it covers. Merge every source and keep the source on each row, so a gap shows.',
'N-R14-03': 'A long-lived key shared by a pipeline can be read by any job on it. One credential per job makes the log name the job that used it.',
'N-R14-04': 'A certificate nobody owns expires without a warning, and the usual fix is to switch the check off. An owner gets the warning at 30 and 7 days.',
'N-R14-05': 'Vendor tokens outlive the vendor\'s care. A 90-day expiry on grants refused a 14-month-old grant even after the tokens were stolen.',
'N-R14-09': 'With no rule, an ownerless key stays active. A rule that pauses it on day 31 turns a silent risk into a ticket someone has to answer.',
'N-R14-11': 'Retiring an application does not retire its identities. The attestation rule closed all three, but only against the list it was given. An incomplete list leaves some open.',
'N-R14-14': 'A rotation job can fail for two months and nobody knows. An alert on day 1 and a score that rises each week make the failure visible.',
'N-R14-15': 'Rotate in the wrong order and the application fails at its next connection. Update the consumer first. A consumer missing from the list still fails.',
'N-R14-17': 'A role that trusts any workload in an account hands a high-value role to a low-value job. Binding trust to one workload refused it.',
'N-R14-18': 'A front door that refuses new identities without an owner, a scope and an expiry stops the problem at creation. That is cheaper than finding it later.',
'P-R15-02': 'Standing access stays after the work is done. A two-hour window closes by itself and the credential is rotated.',
'P-R15-03': 'Extensions can run on and on: 10 hours in the model. One extension, then a named person, stopped that.',
'P-R15-05': 'Of 40 admin group members, 8 did privileged work. Pruning removed 32. Two of the remaining 8 were still broader than they need, so pruning is a start and not an end.',
'P-R15-07': 'A vault does not help while the old password still works. Blocking the direct route at the network closes the old door.',
'P-R15-08': 'Nine recorded targets look like coverage. A check that compares the recording list with the target list found the tenth.',
'P-R15-09': 'A change to the identity system with one approver just runs. Requiring a second, different person made it wait.',
'P-R15-11': 'Break-glass used at 2 a.m. went unnoticed and the password stayed the same. An alert, a written reason and a rotation covered it. The reason is text, and the model does not check it is true.',
'P-R15-13': 'Break-glass can turn into a side door for routine work. A monthly report flagged 2 uses with no ticket. A ticket number is not checked against the ticket system.',
'P-R15-17': 'A vendor session with no record and standing access is the default. A broker that records the session and closes it at 4 hours replaced it.',
'P-R15-18': 'When a vendor\'s engineer leaves their company, your account for them stays. A sponsor review every 30 days disabled it. Without the review it was still active at 120 days.',
'S-R17-01': 'An employee\'s grant to an AI tool kept working after they left. Revoking on leaving and scanning for grants found it, and grants with no owner were refused.',
'S-R17-03': 'An automation that uses a person\'s login makes the log unreadable. A separate agent identity and a log tag tell a human from an agent.',
'S-R17-04': 'AI-assisted workflows can create keys with no owner. Refusing them at creation, and sweeping each week, stopped ghost credentials.',
'S-R17-05': 'The scan blocked 6 of 8 planted secrets. I guessed it would miss 1 and it missed 2: one split over two lines and one in base64. A pattern scan has this limit.',
'N-R14-23': 'Letting every app in lets a breached vendor\'s token in. An allowlist refused the grant. An allowlisted app that is breached still works until you revoke it, and revoking ended replays only after 60 minutes.',
'N-R14-24': 'Secrets in environment variables can sit in plain text. Marking them by name pattern hid 3 of 4. The odd one was missed.',
'N-R14-25': 'A token copied from a CI runner was replayed twice. Binding the token and revoking it refused both. I guessed a short lifetime would matter and it did not, because the binding had already blocked the replay.',
'N-R14-07': 'One account with three names splits findings: 43 entries for 30 identities. Matching on owner and normalised name gave 29, one short, because it merged two real accounts. My own arithmetic in the guess was wrong too.',
'N-R14-19': 'Two identities with the same age score tied. Putting reach into the score ranked the production one first, so it is fixed first.',
'N-R14-20': 'Closing a rotation ticket does not prove the old secret is dead. Of 5 tickets, 2 had old secrets still accepted. Checking first closed 3 and kept 2 open.',
'N-R14-21': 'Four agents on one team key: the log says "team key" and a revoke hits all four. One key per agent makes the log name the agent and the revoke hit one.',
'N-R14-22': 'A person who signs in as a service account is logged under the account. Refusing interactive sign-in and naming the person at checkout fixed accountability. A password copied at checkout and used elsewhere is still not seen.',
'P-R15-10': 'Push fatigue and SIM swap got 6 bad sign-ins through. Passkeys with no SMS fallback let none through.',
'P-R15-15': 'A vault backup on a weaker share undoes the vault\'s rules. The same rules plus an alert on non-backup reads refused 5 reads and raised 5 alerts.',
'P-R15-16': 'A help desk reset path let both attacker resets through. Proof, or a notice with a delay, stopped both. Each control alone changed nothing, because the other already covered it. If the real person is tricked into approving the notice, both controls on still lose the account.',
'P-R15-19': 'A vendor needed at 2 a.m. got a shared password that stayed on. An approved and recorded session that closes at 2 hours replaced it.',
'P-R15-25': 'Rotating 200 secrets after a breach caused 12 outages. Consumers first plus a dry run caused none, and left 2 secrets unrotated for a person to deal with.',
'S-R17-02': 'An AI tool that needs one folder inherited 40. Scoping it to read on one folder left no folder writable.',
'S-R17-06': 'Six AI-service keys sat in six repositories. Scan and revoke removed 5 and missed one with an odd prefix. A 90-day rotation alone changed nothing, because the scan already covered it.',
'S-R17-10': 'Five automations outlived a test. An end date at creation plus a sweep disabled 4. One was extended, and an extension is accepted on any written reason.',
'C-R16-C01': 'Five links from no owner to a sign-in from a new network. The owner rule stops it at the first link. I guessed the ticket scan would matter most, and the owner rule did. For an account that already exists the owner rule cannot apply, and the ticket scan stops it at the second link.',
'C-R16-C02': 'The commit block stops a token at link 1. A one-day token alone also stops the chain when the finder takes 7 days. If the finder is quicker than the token lifetime (12 hours), the short token alone no longer helps.',
'C-R16-C04': 'Expiring the account at the end date stops the chain at link 3. A sponsor review alone leaves a window of up to 90 days. If the attack comes 120 days after the contract ends, the review does stop it.',
'C-R16-C05': 'An alert on every use reports the reuse at link 4 and does not stop it. Rotating the password on return makes the pasted one useless, which stops the chain at link 3.',
'C-R16-C06': 'Showing the risk first flags the hostile request. A second approver for high-risk requests stops it. Whether a tired approver acts on the flag is human behaviour, and this model does not test that.',
'C-R16-C17': 'Blocking direct login stops the chain at link 2. A vault check-in alert only reports it, at link 4, after the bypass has happened.',
}

